<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Barth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Barth was a German explorer and scholar who travelled extensively in North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in the mid-nineteenth century. His detailed accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and neighbouring states documented their trade, society and history. His work remains a valuable source for historians of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth.jpg" alt="Heinrich Barth" width="245" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barth_Unterschrift.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Heinrich Barth was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> on 16 February 1821, the third child of Johann Christoph Heinrich Barth, who had built up a successful trading business, and his wife Charlotte Karoline. Both parents were orthodox Lutherans. He excelled at languages and taught himself some Arabic. In 1839 he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>, where he heard the geographer Karl Ritter and the historian Jakob Grimm, and in July 1844 he defended a doctoral thesis on the trade relations of ancient Corinth.</p><p>In January 1845 he set out on a grand tour that his father funded. After two months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> learning Arabic, he crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in August. He travelled overland across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to Wadi Halfa and on through Sinai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, examining the remains of antiquity everywhere. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> on 27 December 1847.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Prussian ambassador Christian von Bunsen secured places for Barth and the astronomer Adolf Overweg on James Richardson's expedition, which the British government had chosen to open commercial relations with the states of the central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a>. The party left Tripoli early in 1850 and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> with great difficulty. Barth reached Agadez in October 1850, and a memorial tablet there honours him as the first European to enter the city. Richardson died in March 1851 and Overweg in September 1852, leaving Barth to carry on alone.</p><p>He was the first European to visit Adamawa, in 1851, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in September 1853. When he returned to Tripoli in September 1855 his journey had covered upward of 12,000 miles (19,000 km), from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagirmi">Bagirmi</a> in the east to Timbuktu in the west. In Timbuktu his friendship with Ahmad al-Bakkai al-Kunti led to his staying in the man's house and receiving protection from an attempt to seize him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Barth was interested in the history and culture of African peoples rather than in commercial exploitation. He was not the first European to notice local oral traditions, but he was the first to consider seriously their methodology and use for historical research, and he investigated the history of regions such as the Songhay Empire.</p><p>His account, published in English and German as Travels and Discoveries in North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> (1857–1858), runs to five volumes of about 3,500 pages and was cited by Darwin. It gave the first European evidence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> had once been a savanna. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 25 November 1865, aged 44.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich Barth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Commercially, the caliphate's integration of a very large area intensified long-distance trade: Kano cloth was distributed across the Sahara and much of West Africa, and <b>Heinrich Barth</b>'s celebrated travels through the caliphate in the 1850s produced detailed ethnographic and commercial documentation that remains invaluable to historians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Heinrich Barth</b> passed through Dori in 1853 and described the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-liptako">Emirate of Liptako</a></li><li><blockquote>The explorer <b>Heinrich Barth</b> described it in 1851 as the commercial centre of the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/kano-emirate">Kano Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Hamburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4772 323 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heinrich Barth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5222 593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5222 593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
